On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 11:09:55 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> > +mailboxes INBOX, =Next=, =Later=, and =Archive=, as on Fastmail, and
> > +creates all but INBOX if they are missing. Every login sees the same
>
> Why does it create the dirs? I don't think it should need to. Dir
> management should come from Fastmail.

You're right, it doesn't need to. Dovecot created them because v10
also set mbsync to Create None, and that made mbsync skip any folder
missing on lab-mail. With neither of them creating folders, Next,
Later, and Archive would never sync to a fresh maildir. Nobody on the
list had argued for the auto-create. It was a leftover from v1.

v11 fixes it the other way around. Dovecot creates no folder, and
mbsync uses Create Near. It creates on lab-mail any of the four
folders it finds on Fastmail, and never creates a folder on Fastmail.
Patterns still limits the sync to INBOX, Next, Later, and Archive, so
any other folder you make on Fastmail stays there. mbsync's login gets
the ACL's create right (k) back for this, and it is the only login
with that right.

This is posted as "[PATCH v11 00/10] jails/instances/lab-mail: Build
the list's mail hub" [1]. The spec change is in patch 3, and the
config and README.org changes are in patches 5, 6, and 10. Patches 1,
2, 4, 7, 8, and 9 are unchanged. The cover letter has an interdiff
against v10.

Before the first sync you still create Next and Later on Fastmail.
mbsync then brings them, and Archive, over to lab-mail.

Both mbsyncrc files parse with mbsync 1.5.1. Dovecot isn't installed
here, so I haven't checked that k lets mbsync create a top-level
mailbox under acl_globals_only. If the first sync logs a create
failure for Archive, Next, or Later, that is the place to look.

